        'en' => [
            'title'   => 'Choosing a laser engraver for home: a beginner’s guide',
            'slug'    => 'laser-engraver-for-home-beginners',
            'excerpt' => 'A beginner’s guide to home laser engraving: plan the workspace and extraction, compare M2 and S1, and learn why F2 requires professional conditions.',
            'blocks'  => [
                [ 'p', 'For a first laser engraver at home, an enclosed Class 1 diode machine with fume extraction planned in from day one is the most sensible start. In the xTool range, choose the M2 if you also want colour printing and sticker cutting, or the S1 if you mainly engrave and cut wood, plywood and leather on a 608 × 385 mm working area. The F2 can mark metal and jewellery but is a Class 4 laser for professional use; the P3 needs suitable space and extraction.' ],
                [ 'p', 'We don’t compare laser technologies here – diode, CO₂, MOPA and UV lasers are covered in a separate article. This guide is about the practical side of a first purchase: space, smoke, noise, budget, learning and typical mistakes.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'Where should a home laser engraver go?' ],
                [ 'p', 'An open Class 4 engraver can injure eyes and skin, including through reflected beams. It requires a controlled laser area or suitable enclosure, restricted access and safeguards matched to the device; goggles and a separate room alone are not enough. Follow the manufacturer’s instructions and use appropriate eye protection.' ],
                [ 'p', 'A Class 1 enclosure limits access to radiation with the lid properly closed. Whether a home office or hobby room is suitable also depends on extraction, materials, fire protection and building rules. xTool describes the M2 and S1 as Class 1; the S1 also has flame detection, an emergency stop, a lid-open stop and a key lock.' ],
                [ 'p', 'When you measure the spot, allow for the open lid, an exhaust hose to a window or a purifier next to the machine, and access to a socket. A short exhaust hose works better than a long one, so a nearby window is a big plus. We cover the full rules in our article on working safely with a laser.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'Smoke, smell and noise in a flat' ],
                [ 'p', 'The enclosure limits access to radiation but does not remove contaminants. Processing wood, cardboard or leather can produce smoke and harmful fumes; plywood and MDF also contain adhesives. An absence of odour does not prove that air is safe.' ],
                [ 'p', 'Outdoor extraction needs proper design and an outlet permitted by local rules. A HEPA and activated carbon purifier can reduce some pollutants, but does not remove every gas and is not always suitable for recirculation. Match the system to the material, machine and room; no odour does not mean the air is safe.' ],
                [ 'h3', 'Is a laser engraver loud?' ],
                [ 'p', 'The beam itself is silent; what you hear are the head motors, the fans, the air assist (if fitted) and the extraction. xTool states a noise level for its desktop purifier for the M2 and F2 – no more than 55 dB – but the descriptions of the engravers themselves give no such figure, and for the large P3 xTool even sells an air pump silencer. If quiet matters, listen to the machine in a demo.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'How much to budget for a first laser engraver' ],
                [ 'ul', [
                    'Entry level: the xTool M2, the most affordable laser in our catalogue; the price rises with power (10, 20 or 40 W) and equipment, such as the CMYK module in the Color Print & Cut bundle.',
                    'Mid range: the enclosed S1 (20 or 40 W); check current prices and bundles.',
                    'High end: the P3 costs many times more; it is an investment for batch cutting of large sheets.',
                ] ],
                [ 'p', 'Add extraction or a purifier, test materials and possibly a rotary attachment. The product cards show current prices, and we cover purchase and running costs in our article on how much a laser engraver costs.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'Comparing machines: M2, S1, P3 and the professional F2' ],
                [ 'h3', 'xTool M2 – crafting, printing and cutting in a Class 1 enclosure' ],
                [ 'p', 'The M2 is an enclosed craft machine that, according to xTool, prints, cuts and engraves a wide range of materials. It has a 10, 20 or 40 W diode laser, optional colour printing with the CMYK inkjet module and two cameras with live preview. xTool lists stickers, tattoos, decor and gifts as typical uses. Without the CMYK module it does not print, and like any diode laser it cannot cut clear acrylic.' ],
                [ 'h3', 'xTool S1 – an enclosed diode laser with a large working area' ],
                [ 'p', 'Choose the S1 for wood, plywood, leather and dark acrylic. It has a 20 or 40 W diode laser, switchable modules (including a 1064 nm infrared one), a 608 × 385 mm working area and Pin-point™ positioning, which xTool says is ten times more accurate than single-camera systems. AutoPassthrough™ handles material longer than the bed, and 3D Curve™ engraves curved surfaces. It does not print in colour.' ],
                [ 'p', 'The S1 with a riser base supporting a conveyor does not operate under Class 1 conditions. In that configuration, follow xTool’s Class 4 procedures and safeguards.' ],
                [ 'products', '15452856942805,8169201631445' ],
                [ 'h3', 'xTool F2 – a portable dual laser for metal and small items' ],
                [ 'p', 'The F2 combines a 5 W infrared laser for metals and plastics with a 15 W diode laser that engraves and cuts wood, acrylic and leather. It has a 50 MP camera and speeds of up to 6,000 mm/s. This is a Class 4 laser intended by xTool for professional use: the manufacturer requires training, connected extraction and goggles protecting against all device wavelengths. The Slide Extension prevents the enclosure closing fully, so a controlled laser area and appropriate safeguards are required. It suits jewellery, tags and keyrings, but bigger items need the Slide Extension, and deep engraving of metal at 5 W takes a long time.' ],
                [ 'h3', 'xTool P3 – a large CO₂ laser: when does it make sense at home?' ],
                [ 'p', 'The P3 is an 80 W CO₂ laser with a 914 × 457 mm bed, speeds of up to 1,200 mm/s, four cameras for positioning and safety monitoring, autofocus and LiDAR ranging. Unlike a diode, it cuts clear acrylic. At home it makes sense with a garage or a separate workshop, a strong exhaust to the outside and plans for batch cutting. For your first keyrings it is too much machine, and it cannot mark bare metal without the optional 5 W infrared module.' ],
                [ 'products', '9163846877397,9089720189141' ],
